<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helsinki Final Act</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Helsinki Accords</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Helsinki Final Act was signed on 1 August 1975 in Helsinki by 35 states, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and almost all European countries, at the end of the Conference on Security and Co-operation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It recognised the inviolability of post-war borders and committed the signatories to respect human rights, giving dissidents in the Eastern bloc a basis for their demands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helsinki-final-act.jpg" alt="Helsinki Final Act" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rabax63 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlussakte_Helsinki_1975_(KSZE).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Final Act was the document signed at the closing meeting of the third phase of the Conference on Security and Co-operation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, held in Helsinki between 30 July and 1 August 1975, after two years of negotiations known as the Helsinki Process. All then-existing European countries except Andorra and Hoxhaist Albania signed it, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>,to improve détente. It did not have treaty status and did not need ratification by parliaments, so it was not binding. Henry Kissinger told Gerald Ford in August 1974 that the United States had never wanted the conference but had gone along with the Europeans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The Final Act was arranged in four groupings called baskets. The first, the Declaration on Principles Guiding Relations between Participating States, set out ten points, among them sovereign equality, refraining from the threat or use of force, inviolability of frontiers, territorial integrity, non-intervention in internal affairs, respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms, and equal rights and self-determination of peoples. The second basket promised economic, scientific and technological cooperation. The third dealt with family reunions, marriages and travel, the conditions of journalists and cultural exchanges. The fourth set out procedures to monitor implementation and to plan future meetings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> sought a ban on radio jamming but found no consensus because of Soviet opposition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Many Americans of Eastern European descent feared that the agreement would accept Soviet domination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> and the forced incorporation of the Baltic States. Ford told a group of them in July 1975 that American policy on the Baltic States would not change, and that the Final Act denied annexation of territory in violation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a> made the accords a centrepiece of his campaign against Ford in the 1976 Republican primaries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-carter">Jimmy Carter</a> attacked them as a legitimation of Soviet domination of Eastern Europe.</p><p>The document was seen both as a step toward reducing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> tensions and as a diplomatic boost for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, because of its clauses on frontiers. Its human rights provisions nevertheless became the basis for the work of the Helsinki Watch, which monitored compliance and evolved into the International Helsinki Federation and Human Rights Watch. The historian John Lewis Gaddis wrote that the accords gradually became a manifesto of the dissident and liberal movement. They also served as the groundwork for the Organization for Security and Cooperation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helsinki Accords” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Helsinki hosted the Olympic Games of 1952 and the Conference on Security and Co-operation in Europe, whose <b>Helsinki Final Act</b> of 1975 set principles on borders and human rights that later helped dissidents across the Eastern bloc.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
